WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.880
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, we had a lot of great talks about skills.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.200
There are now millions of them out there, and the honest conclusion from the stage

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.120
was that we have built

4
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.800
an enormous amount of something we don't yet know how to manage.

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.400
Let's first hear from Guy Podjarny, CEO and co-founder of Tessl, who lays out

6
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.000
why skills deserve to be treated

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.560
as code, and sketches out the stack forming around them.

8
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.760
I am Guy, or Guy.

9
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.040
I'm the CEO and founder here at Tessl, as Simon just said,

10
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.360
and I'm excited for two days of sharing and learning.

11
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.720
And I do love the hallway track.

12
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.000
I love the talks on it, but I just love the conversations and the learnings.

13
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.320
And I'd love for you to to share some of those learnings.

14
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.240
You're here to to collaborate, not just to talk.

15
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:44.920
And indeed,

16
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.360
I want to talk to you about sort of why skills, I believe, are the new code.

17
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.280
So we

18
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.160
founded Tessl two plus years ago, and we did it out of the belief

19
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.800
that software development is transforming from revolving around code

20
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.200
and instructions to revolving around intent.

21
00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.200
Sorry. Let me try that again.

22
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.320
Revolving around code

23
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.760
and implementation to revolving around intent and instructions.

24
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.800
And I think that's far

25
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.640
less controversial today. Two years ago, we believed that there's a new dev paradigm

26
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.560
to be had around it, but we didn't know quite what it would look like.

27
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.960
And I think today we're starting to see that sort of new development

28
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.720
stack come into view, which I find quite exciting.

29
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.800
And so we're seeing kind of a bit of a layered software stack.

30
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.200
At the bottom are the new primitives, the models that we're all building on.

31
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.760
Clearly, they are the new superpower

32
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.920
that we've received and we're trying to build for those.

33
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.440
On top of those are tools, which I'll talk about some more,

34
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.760
which help turn models into agents, giving them arms and legs to be able

35
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.880
to affect the world and gather information.

36
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.480
There's context that guides those models as well.

37
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.280
And then increasingly, there is a harness that constrains the model

38
00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.800
or packages a lot of things together.

39
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.480
I'll talk about these three layers in more depth in a section,

40
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.560
and then harnesses compose into factory lines that combine them

41
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.640
all and create these pipelines and into full-on factories.

42
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.520
So we also heard from James Moss, who works on the registry at Tessl.

43
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.720
He gets specific about how skills sprawl actually fails

44
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.640
teams: building the same skill twice without knowing, skills drifting out

45
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.920
of date, and nobody able to tell whether any of them are being used at all.

46
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.000
So how does this sprawl manifest

47
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.840
actually, and impact humans and the agents that we're instructing?

48
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.600
So there's a couple of different failure modes that we're seeing.

49
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.080
The first one is overlap.

50
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.640
So this is where you might have multiple teams all building the same thing

51
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.840
in isolation, without realizing. Each team might have built their own version

52
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.640
of the skill. It might achieve the same thing.

53
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.120
You might have

54
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.120
generally the same outcomes, but it's done in a different way, right?

55
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.120
And you're having multiple kind of wasted effort there.

56
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.440
So that's overlap.

57
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.400
Next up we often see drift.

58
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.520
So this is where newer skills are shipping.

59
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.880
And teams aren't keeping up with those newer versions.

60
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.520
And a good example here recently is Matt Pocock.

61
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.640
Hopefully you've all heard of him.

62
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.560
He's a great educator, published a bunch of great skills.

63
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.480
He has a Grill Me skill.

64
00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.200
That's where you kind of do an interview with the agent and you kind of

65
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.480
get a shared understanding.

66
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.080
He recently published a newer version of that Grill skill, called Grill

67
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.480
with Docs, which kind of expands on it and adds a lot more detail to it.

68
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.400
I'm sure there's lots of folks that don't realize that that's been released, right?

69
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.520
And they're still using that older version and haven't updated.

70
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.280
So that's drift.

71
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.440
We also have problems around activation, or a lack of activation, right?

72
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.040
Lots of people are producing these skills.

73
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.480
Are they being used by the agents?

74
00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.080
Are they being used by humans?

75
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.720
There's no real way to to understand that

76
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.720
and know that right now. You probably don't have that visibility yet.

77
00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.360
So I mentioned this in his talk as well.

78
00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.520
This morning.

79
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.960
You might have a skill that describes some code or a code base, or a process

80
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.720
or even a workflow, and those two things can quickly go out of sync,

81
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.040
so your skill never gets updated. Things change.

82
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.040
And in those instances, having an outdated skill

83
00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.280
can often be just as bad as having no skill at all.

84
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:19.360
Right?

85
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.520
John Groetzinger is a principal engineer at Cisco, and he has a line

86
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.480
I've thought a lot about

87
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.640
since: you don't need a smarter model, you need smarter context.

88
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.840
He went and tested it and found mid-tier models could do the job

89
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.640
once the context was right.

90
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.400
So this might be a little controversial

91
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.600
for some people here, especially maybe if you work for a frontier model

92
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.040
company — earmuffs — but you don't really need a smarter model today.

93
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.000
The models are perfectly capable of doing what you need for business value, right?

94
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.360
Not talking like PhD level intelligence and beyond, but getting business value

95
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.360
out of the models. It's already there today.

96
00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.760
The problem is really that you need

97
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.760
smarter context and smarter context engineering.

98
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.280
And so for me, the last couple of years I've been jumping harness to harness.

99
00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.840
I'm always chasing the latest, greatest thing, constantly breaking my workflows.

100
00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.600
I'm sure other people can relate to this.

101
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.640
And always there's so many variables that anytime I switch to a lower model

102
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.560
and it breaks something, I'm immediately like, well, that's the model's fault.

103
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:18.680
I'm going back to Opus, right?

104
00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.880
Because this thing that I don't understand, it has to be the model, right?

105
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.000
But once skills came onto the scene, it kind of changed my

106
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.560
perspective on things, especially recently with the new patterns

107
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.560
of agentic fan out in the harness, right, where I have my model set to Opus,

108
00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.800
and I ask it to do this massive prompt and it spawns 15 subagents.

109
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.240
That cost is just really increasing, and we're actually seeing that becoming

110
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.280
actually a concern where we had to start limiting model use on our large enterprise

111
00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.520
because of the cost of this stuff and the fan out being a problem.

112
00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.480
So I really wanted to figure out,

113
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.760
okay, why can't I just use Sonnet for everything?

114
00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.400
And I found out I can.

115
00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.640
And today, for the last couple of months, I largely use the medium tier model.

116
00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:58.640
Whether that's Sonnet,

117
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.800
GPT medium reasoning — I don't really use those high end models.

118
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.720
It's very rare that I use those.

119
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.440
Maybe for some super complex planning. Lots of context.

120
00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.440
I still use them, but I challenge my engineers to really try

121
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.040
to use that medium tier as their baseline and only jump into the greater tier

122
00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:13.800
when they need to.

123
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.040
And the real unlock for this was skills, because all the harnesses,

124
00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.800
all the models are training on skills, they're honoring skills.

125
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.640
And so if you can lean into that,

126
00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.560
your context can actually transfer between the harnesses.

127
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.400
And that was great for me because I can

128
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:31.520
I can try Claude Code, I can jump into dev and I can jump into GitHub Copilot CLI.

129
00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.080
And if my skills work in all three of them relatively, it works the same.

130
00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.640
So it's great.

131
00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.640
But what you need to understand is how do you manage your context, right?

132
00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.600
How do you share it?

133
00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.440
Package it once

134
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.000
and then ship it to everyone, but only have one source of management.

135
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.880
And the real unlock for that is evals.

136
00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.520
And finally, Rob Willoughby and Simon Obstbaum

137
00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.520
from the Stanford lab, who actually measured this:

138
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.520
500 skills, a thousand tasks,

139
00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.680
19 combinations of model and harness.

140
00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:04.360
Their

141
00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.120
most uncomfortable finding is that more than half the time, the model

142
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.120
followed the skill's instructions, even when the skill was not loaded at all.

143
00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.000
the theorem.

144
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.560
I want to talk a little bit about what kind of task

145
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.320
and what I mean by full completion and instruction

146
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:24.280
following. So what we're running is we've got 500 skills.

147
00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.360
We have 1000 tasks.

148
00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.080
We've got 19, 19 permutations of models and harnesses.

149
00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.600
So we got 19 permutations of different models, different harnesses,

150
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.480
because those have an effect on each other and also affect the performance.

151
00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:42.640
And then the tasks that we're using, those are synthetic.

152
00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.880
But they're anchored on the skill itself, they're meant to be something

153
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.560
that you could expect the skill to trigger for.

154
00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.640
So if you have a skill that says, how do you implement

155
00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.720
API security, the task that we're going to construct is that

156
00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:57.200
great.

157
00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.120
Or change the authentication from password to some other mechanism for that,

158
00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.560
or something else that might not be related,

159
00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.920
to expect them to be picking up on the security and looking for that.

160
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.320
And so hopefully potentially not trying to do

161
00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.320
like super hard pushing boundaries here.

162
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:19.840
Think of it as kind of like well scoped to your tickets and expecting engineers

163
00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.160
to be getting that one.

164
00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.560
And so we see that

165
00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.880
hitting a threshold of 1,993% of us,

166
00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:30.840
whether it's in their office in holding, this is the metrics

167
00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.240
that are specifically in the agenda, what they do.

168
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.520
So if you have your own internal design for how you want to do whatever you hold

169
00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.760
or to make sure that you're updating two supervision versus another,

170
00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:44.040
that's the information that we put in the skill

171
00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.720
and then encoded in the instruction following improvement that we see there.

172
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.760
One really interesting thing about that number specifically

173
00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.680
is that we see 55% following the instructions of the skill,

174
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.800
even when the skill is not present.

175
00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.520
That means the information that is encoded in the skill,

176
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.000
that's actually in the weights of the model already.

177
00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.320
And so when you're talking to you, it was getting rid of anyway.

178
00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:08.480
So that means that's actually valuable because burning inference

179
00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.600
tokens and paying money down in Google

180
00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.960
when the model is going to get in ways that they can get to do that.

181
00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.160
So you're giving this will be finishing.

182
00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.600
But the things that you care about, the structure of these skills

183
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.920
or changes, how it does it and how well it does it.

184
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.080
Thanks for watching.

